1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:22,060
In 2007, I decided that we needed to re-conceptualize how we thought about economic development.

2
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:27,100
Our new goal should be that when every family thinks about where they want to live and work,

3
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:32,860
they should be able to choose between at least a handful of different cities that were all

4
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:36,620
competing to attract new residents.

5
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:39,180
Now we're a long way away from that goal right now.

6
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:43,140
There are billions of people in developing countries who don't have even a single city

7
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:45,340
that would be willing to welcome them.

8
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:50,460
But the amazing thing about cities is they're worth so much more than it costs to build

9
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:51,460
them.

10
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:57,540
So we could easily supply the world with dozens, maybe hundreds of new cities.

11
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:01,500
Now this might sound preposterous to you if you've never thought about new cities, but

12
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,500
just substitute apartment building for cities.

13
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:07,260
Imagine half the people who wanted to be in apartments already had them.

14
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:09,120
The other half aren't there yet.

15
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:15,160
You could try and expand the capacity by doing additions on all the existing apartments,

16
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:18,780
but you know what you'd run into is those apartments in the surrounding areas have rules

17
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:23,740
to avoid discomfort and the distractions of construction.

18
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:27,220
So it's extremely hard to do all of those additions, but you could go out someplace

19
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:32,720
brand new, build a brand new apartment building as long as the rules there were ones which

20
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:36,820
facilitated construction rather than getting in the way.

21
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:43,500
So I proposed that governments create new reform zones big enough to hold cities and

22
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:46,980
gave them a name, charter cities.

23
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:52,100
Later I learned that about this same time, Javier and Octavio were thinking about the

24
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:55,140
challenge of reform in Honduras.

25
00:01:55,140 --> 00:02:01,100
They knew that about 75,000 Hondurans every year would leave to go to the United States,

26
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:05,500
and they wanted to ask what could they do to make sure that those people could stay

27
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:07,580
and do the same things in Honduras.

28
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:12,420
At one point, Javier said to Octavio, what if we took some of our empty land, what if

29
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:16,700
we just gave it to an embassy, give some to the US embassy, give some to the Canadian

30
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:20,580
embassy, and then if people want to go work under the rules of Canada or the rules of

31
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:25,340
the United States, they could go get jobs, do everything they do on those embassy grounds

32
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:30,140
that they would otherwise have to go to Canada or the US to do.

33
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:35,140
In the summer of 2009, Honduras went through a wrenching constitutional crisis.

34
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:41,020
At the next regularly scheduled election, Pepe Lobo won in a landslide on a platform

35
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:46,260
that promised reform but reconciliation as well.

36
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:49,540
He asked Octavio to be his chief of staff.

37
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:54,020
Meanwhile, I was getting ready to give a talk at TED Global.

38
00:02:54,020 --> 00:02:59,380
Through a process of refinement, trial and error, a lot of user testing, I tried to boil

39
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:05,700
this complicated concept of a charter city down to the bare essentials.

40
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:10,260
The first point was the importance of rules, like those rules that say you can't come in

41
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:12,860
and disturb all the existing apartment holders.

42
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:16,940
We pay a lot of attention to new technologies, but it takes technologies and rules to get

43
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:21,860
progress and it's usually the rules that hold us back.

44
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:28,100
In the fall of 2010, a friend from Guatemala sent Octavio a link to the TED Talk.

45
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:34,560
He showed it to Javier, they called me, they said, let's present this to the leaders of

46
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:35,560
our country.

47
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:40,040
So in December, we met in Miami in a hotel conference room.

48
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:45,000
I tried to explain this point about how valuable cities are, how much more valuable they are

49
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,540
than they cost, and I used this slide showing how valuable the raw land is in a place like

50
00:03:50,540 --> 00:03:51,540
New York City.

51
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:57,280
Notice, land that's worth thousands of dollars in some cases per square meter.

52
00:03:57,280 --> 00:04:03,880
But it was a fairly abstract discussion and at some point when there was a pause, Octavio

53
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:09,280
said, Paul, maybe we could watch the TED Talk.

54
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:15,040
So the TED Talk laid out in very simple terms, a charter city is a place where you start

55
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:21,540
with uninhabited land, a charter that specifies the rules that will apply there, and then

56
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:27,140
a chance for people to opt in, to go live under those rules or not.

57
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:33,020
So I was asked by the president of Honduras, who said that we need to do this project,

58
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:36,760
this is important, this could be the way forward for our country.

59
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:41,940
I was asked to come to Tegucigalpa and talk again on January 4th and 5th.

60
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:48,140
So I presented another fact-filled lecture that included a slide like this, which tried

61
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:51,980
to make the point that if you want to create a lot of value in a city, it has to be very

62
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:52,980
big.

63
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:57,200
This is a picture of Denver, and the outline is the new airport that was built in Denver.

64
00:04:57,200 --> 00:05:01,620
This airport alone covers more than 100 square kilometers.

65
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:05,140
So I was trying to persuade the Hondurans, if you build a new city, you've got to start

66
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:08,440
with a site that's at least 1,000 square kilometers.

67
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:14,640
That's more than 250-100,000 acres.

68
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:16,380
Everybody applauded politely.

69
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:20,760
The faces in the audience were very serious and attentive.

70
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:25,280
The leader of the Congress came up on stage and said, Professor Romer, thank you very

71
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:30,300
much for our lecture, but maybe we could watch the TED Talk.

72
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:33,720
I've got it here on my laptop.

73
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:38,700
So I sat down, and they played the TED Talk, and it got to the essence, which is that a

74
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:41,700
new city could offer new choices for people.

75
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:45,940
There'd be a choice of a city you could go to, which could be in Honduras, instead of

76
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:48,260
hundreds of miles away in the north.

77
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:52,380
And it also involved new choices for leaders, because the leaders in the government there

78
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:56,500
in Honduras would need help from partner countries or could benefit from partner countries who

79
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:02,220
help them set up the rules in this charter and the enforcement so everybody can trust

80
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:05,120
that the charter really will be enforced.

81
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:10,740
And the insight of President Lobo was that that assurance of enforcement that I was thinking

82
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:15,000
about as a way to get the foreign investors to come in and build the city could be equally

83
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,720
important for all the different parties in Honduras who had suffered for so many years

84
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:22,600
from fear and distrust.

85
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:24,040
We went and looked at a site.

86
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:25,040
This picture is from there.

87
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:28,800
It easily could hold 1,000 square kilometers.

88
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:34,780
And shortly thereafter, on January 19th, they voted in the Congress to amend their constitution

89
00:06:34,780 --> 00:06:38,780
to have a constitutional provision that allows for special development regions.

90
00:06:38,780 --> 00:06:43,100
In a country which had just gone through this wrenching crisis, the vote in the Congress

91
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:47,980
in favor of this constitutional amendment was 124 to 1.

92
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:51,360
All parties, all factions in society backed this.

93
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:54,140
To be part of the Constitution, you actually have to pass it twice in the Congress.

94
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:59,840
On February 17th, they passed it again with another vote of 114 to 1.

95
00:06:59,840 --> 00:07:06,280
Immediately after that vote, on February 21st to the 24th, a delegation of about 30 Hondurans

96
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:10,520
went to the two places in the world that are most interested in getting into the city building

97
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:11,880
business.

98
00:07:11,880 --> 00:07:13,280
One is South Korea.

99
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:17,220
This is a picture of a big new city center that's being built in South Korea, bigger

100
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:19,140
than downtown Boston.

101
00:07:19,140 --> 00:07:22,600
Everything you see there was built in four years after they spent four years getting

102
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:24,660
the permits.

103
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:27,420
The other place that's very interested in city building is Singapore.

104
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:31,600
They've actually built two cities already in China and are preparing the third.

105
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:34,540
So if you think about this practically, here's where we are.

106
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:35,820
They've got a site.

107
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:38,540
They're already thinking about the site for the second city.

108
00:07:38,540 --> 00:07:42,940
They're putting place a legal system that could allow for managers to come in and also

109
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:44,240
an external legal system.

110
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:48,180
One country has already volunteered to let its Supreme Court be the court of final appeal

111
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:50,400
for the new judicial system there.

112
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:53,640
There's designers and builders of cities who are very interested.

113
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:56,080
They even can bring with them some financing.

114
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:59,980
But the one thing you know that they've already solved is that there's lots of tenants.

115
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:03,700
There's lots of businesses that would like to locate in the Americas, especially in a

116
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:05,520
place with a free trade zone.

117
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:08,220
And there's lots of people who'd like to go there.

118
00:08:08,220 --> 00:08:12,100
Around the world, there's 700 million people who say they'd like to move permanently someplace

119
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:13,380
else right now.

120
00:08:13,380 --> 00:08:17,340
There's a million a year who leave Latin America to go to the United States.

121
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:21,440
Many of these are a father who has to leave his family behind to go get a job, sometimes

122
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:26,080
a single mother who has to go to get enough money to even pay for food or clothing.

123
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:30,360
Sadly, sometimes there are even children who are trying to get reunited with their parents

124
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:34,920
that they haven't seen in some cases for a decade.

125
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:39,480
So what kind of an idea is it to think about building a brand new city in Honduras or to

126
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:43,200
build a dozen of these or a hundred of these around the world?

127
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:48,300
What kind of an idea is it to think about insisting that every family have a choice

128
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:53,820
of several cities that are competing to attract new residents?

129
00:08:53,820 --> 00:08:56,280
This is an idea worth spreading.

130
00:08:56,280 --> 00:08:59,380
My friends from Honduras asked me to say, thank you, Ted.

